  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\n   The current optimization strategy relies heavily on multi-phase L-BFGS-B with heuristic initial patterns. To escape local optima, we propose:\n\n   - **Differentiation by Coarse-to-Fine Strategy**: Start from a very coarse resolution (2 samples) and incrementally refine to the target (560 samples). This leverages:\n\n     * **Early Exploration**: Finding candidate structures faster at coarse resolution\n     * **Dimensionality Reduction**: Avoiding the curse of dimensionality in high-resolution spaces\n     * **Robust Convergence**: Escaping local minima by n jumping between scales\n\n   - **Multi-Scale Architecture**: Optimizations proceed through progressive resolution increases: n \u2208 {4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512}\n\n   The bottleneck addressed: Ineffective exploration in high-dimensional optimization spaces (560 dimensions) leads to suboptimal solutions. This multi-phase approach ensures more comprehensive search through scale space.\n\n   Important limitations:\n\n   * Requires maintaining representations at different resolutions\n   - Current formulations may have higher memory footprints\n   - Simple interpolation may not preserve function properties optimally\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\n   Objective:\n   \ufffd\ufffd\ufffd -> min max_{k\u2208Z}\\int_{0}^{2} h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n   Subject to:\n     (a) 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n     (b) \u222b_{0}^{2} h(x)dx = 1\n\n   Implementation:\n\n   (a) Representation:\n\n        At resolution n, h = [h\u2080, h\u2081, ..., h_{n-1}], each h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n\n        Maintaining overlapping regions between consecutive scales ensures properties propagate.\n\n   (b) Progressive Refinement:\n\n        Stage 0 (Base): n_base = 2\n        Stage k: double resolution (up to n_final = 560)\n\n        h^{2} \u2192 h^{4} \u2192 ... \u2192 h^{512} \u2192 h^{560}\n\n        h^{n} derived from h^{2n} via: \n            h^{n} = projection(upsample(h^{2n}, factor=2)[:, :n])\n\n        However, transforms to interference-free scaling are necessary. Use:\n\n            h_intermediate(i) = average [h^{2n}_{2i}, h^{2n}_{2i+1}]\n\n   (c) Coherence Constraints:\n\n        Preserve:\n          \u222b h(x)dx \u2248 1 (within tolerance)\n          0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\n        At each stage, repair using normalized trimming/linear adjustment:\n          h_i = projection( h_i * s / \u222bh_i dx ) where s is scaling factor from base resolution.\n\n   (d) Optimization:\n\n        At each scale factor of 2, use the following mixed methods sequentially:\n\n          1. Orthogonal/Random Basis Exploration: Sample directions from combinations of:\n             - Shift-optimized basis\n             - Symmetric+antisymmetric splits\n             - Local variance components\n\n          2. Guided Local Search: Apply gradient-based methods from multiple initialization points\n\n        Penalize components that produce: \n          - Boundary artifacts\n          - Unnatural oscillations (spatial derivatives)\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\n   (a) Phased Multi-scale Toolkit:\n\n        def coscale(h_in, factor=2):\n            \"\"\"Coarse-scale representation (minimal version)\"\"\"\n            n = len(h_in)//factor\n            if factor not in [2,4]:  # only double for interpolation stability\n                return np.mean(h_in.reshape(-1, factor)[:n], axis=1)\n\n        def refine(h_in, factor=2):\n            \"\"\"Rise resolution\"\"\"\n            n_base = len(h_in)\n            n_out = n_base*factor\n            return np.zeros(n_out)\n\n        def repair(h):\n            \"\"\"Adaptive rescaler and clipper\"\"\"\n            target = len(h)/2 * (2/len(h))  # implicit integral condition: sum(h)\u2248target_sum, defined as n_points/2\n            current_sum = np.sum(h)\n            scale = target / current_sum if current_sum!=0 else 1\n            h_scaled = h * scale\n            return np.clip(h_scaled, 0, 1)\n\n        def adaptive_penalty(h, base_h):\n            \"\"\"Penalty for violation of multi-scale consistency\"\"\"\n            penalty = 0\n            for base_val in base_h:\n                # Compute residues through local consistency\n                # ... (skipped for brevity)\n            return penalty\n\n        (b) Resolution Jump Strategy:\n\n            scales = [2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512,560]\n            Parent = {}\n            Stage from low to high resolution:\n\n            for factor in [2,4,...,256]:\n                n_coarse, n_fine = factor, factor*2\n                # 0. Initialize using parent\n                h_fine = np.zeros(n_fine)\n\n                # 1. Exploration: Poisson-based initialization\n                W = np.random.randn(n_fine)*3 - 1  # three standard deviations\n                h_base = repair(W)\n\n                # 2. Base projection for score computation\n                parent_coarse = coscale(h_base, factor/2) if factor>2 else initial\n\n                # 3. if ratio of current resolution to base is power of 2, \n                h_fine = refine(parent_coarse,2)  # ensure matching shape\n\n                # 4. Periodic extension if required\n                h_per = np.tile(h_fine, 2)  # forced periodicity initially\n\n                # 5. Objective function wrapper\n                def obj(x): \n                    corr = np.correlate(x,1-x, mode='full')\n                    return (np.max(corr) * dx)  # dx=2/len(x)\n\n                # 6. Standardize gradients\n                def grad(x):\n                    corr = np.correlate(x,1-x, mode='full')\n                    # gradient computation... (omitted)\n\n                # 7. Combined optimizer\n                lbfgs_result = minimize(grad, repair_poisson_sample, method='L-BFGS-B', bounds=(0,1))\n                h_fine_opt = repair(lbfgs_result.x)\n\n                # 8. Record hyperband accuracy\n                score = obj(h_fine_opt)\n\n                # 9. Set parent for next resolution\n                Parent[n_fine] = h_fine_opt\n\n            (c) Hyperparameter Sweep:\n\n                n_bootstraps = 3\n                grid_search = {'alpha':[1.5,2.0,2.5], 'm':[15,20,30], 'ftol':[1e-8,1e-9,1e-10]}\n\n                For each parameter combination, optimize the L-BFGS-B probabilities.\n\n   (d) Candidate Acceptance:\n        Track best_score across all scales.\n        Use patience-based stopping, with history_saved = 5 best candidates.\n\n   (e) Final Resolution Re-finement:\n        After reaching 560, fix parent representations and allow final polishing.\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n\n   (a) Resolution Progression:\n\n        Start at scale 2, double each time. Stop at scale 560.\n\n        n_stages = log2(280) \u2248 8.12 \u2192 9 stages\n\n        Computation estimate: Each stage requires up to 50 gradient evaluations, batch_factor=3 \u2192 405 evaluations total.\n\n   (b) Optimization Dynamics:\n\n        Phase I: Randomized search in projected space (10 Steps)\n        Phase II: Adaptive L-BFGS with penalization (100 Steps)\n        Phase III: Smoothed gradient descent (50 Steps)\n\n        Intervals require monitoring of cross-validation scores.\n\n   (c) Selection Dynamics:\n\n        Composite selection based on:\n          - Function value\n          - Multi-scale consistency score\n          - Coherence with all parent representations\n\n   (d) Variations:\n\n        1. Stochastic Resampling: For flat regions, inject Poisson noise in initialization.\n        2. Progressive Smoothness: Gradually increase constraint on smoothness across scales.\n        3. Delayed Refinement: When parent improvement stalls, temporarily jump resolutions.\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\n   (a) Theoretical Estimate:\n\n        Stage resolution n: O(n) function evaluations.\n        Total computation: O(n_max^2) in worst-case.\n        With n_max=560, worst-case \u0334313600 evaluations, too costly for 1100s.\n\n   (b) Reality:\n\n        - lbfgs evaluations:  \u033410,000 total for all stages.\n        - Rhythmic sleep intervals ensure real-time progression.\n        - Adaptive step sizing and dimensionality reduction (by scale) crucial.\n\n   (c) Memory: Track and store all parent representations. \n        For n_max=560, each is 560-vector, 9 stages \u2192 5040 values (\u2248 5040*80B = 403.2GB) - \n\n        Wait, this exceeds memory capacity. We need scalable storage:\n\n        (i) Use hierarchical storage: parents for stage n are derived from stage n/2\n        (ii) Only keep two adjacent stages active at any time (n and 2n)\n        (iii) Use interpolated storage for coarse-to-fine\n\n   (d) Smart Pruning:\n\n        - Maintain a rolling history of candidates (top 10)\n        - Skip refinements if objective can be computed faster\n        - Exploit fact that most refined stages yield marginal gains\n\n   (e) Goal: Complete all stages in under 1000s with patience-based stopping.\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\n   (a) Feasibility Checks:\n\n        - Repair_module ensures 0\u2264x_i\u22641\n        - Multi-scale consistency ensures shapes translate between resolutions\n        - Explicit checks on integral constraints\n\n   (b) Objective Reference:\n\n        final_score = obj(runout_h)\n\n        Use global correlation function directly.\n\n   (c) Code Structure:\n        - keep tight the resolution progression\n        - ensure parent resampling only at power-of-two factors\n        - explicit gradient functions and repair mechanisms\n\n        The returned h_values: 560-vector\n\n        C5_constrained = max(np.correlate(h_values, 1-h_values, mode='full')) * 2.0/560\n\n   (d) Potential breakdown points:\n\n        1. Complex representation management\n        2. Memory constraints at full resolution\n        3. Objective function precision issues\n\n        Treat these with:\n\n          (i) Memory: Use generators and iterative computation\n          (ii) Stability: Scale factors in all transforms\n          (iii) Damping: Time-stamped model restarts\n\n   This plan provides a systematic coarse-to-fine optimization while respecting multi-scale consistency, addressing known pitfalls of the current coarse-grain method.",
